{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342206,"nonce":"+8Jmjztql/tAFQfmlzADFJklcSs=","title":"LLVM's static initializers and sparse compiler text set the start-up RSS floor","message":"A build with llvm-backend runs 642 C++ static initializers at every start, spread over 422 distinct 16 KB text pages (Mac) or 248 64 KB fault-around windows (Linux), even when the program never reaches the LLVM tier. The pipeline's own code (about 3.9 MB of functions for print(1)) is touched sparsely across a 67 MB __text.\n\nEvidence. Mac, lldb census at main(): RSS 16.4 MB, footprint 3.6, __TEXT resident 9.4 before any Rust code; print(1) RSS 43, footprint 20, __TEXT 19. NUC, d7500d72 light vs LLVM: `zylua backend` 4.5 vs 25 to 36 MB, print(1) 28 to 31 vs 50 to 62 MB, zypy 24.4 vs 53.3; pages executed before main 3 vs 869; `backend` wall 1.5 vs 6.9 ms. Relinked with --symbol-ordering-file of the init symbols (3395 names): init pages 869 -\u003e 308, print(1) 36.6 to 37.8 MB, backend 5.6 ms; plus a function-level order from a gdb coverage run: light print(1) 28 to 30.7 -\u003e 22.4 to 22.9 MB, LLVM 33.6 to 33.9. A page-level order had no effect.\n\nSavings: Linux 13 to 21 MB (LLVM init) plus 4 to 8 MB (function order) per process, zylua and zypy alike, and about 1.3 ms of start-up wall; Mac about 11 MB RSS of which about 2 MB footprint (unmeasured with an order file).\n\nFix: a checked-in order file of the init symbols (C++ names carry no crate hash) passed with -order_file on the Mac and --symbol-ordering-file on Linux, then a release step that profiles the executed functions per binary. Longer term, the LLVM tier as a library loaded at the first promotion. Package MEM-startup-order.","files":null}]}